      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We can start by using the fact that \\[\\sin(x+y)=\\sin(x)\\cos(y)+\\sin(y)\\cos(x)\\]\nThus, we need to show that \\[\\sin(x)\\cos(y)+\\sin(y)\\cos(x)\\leq \\sin(x)+\\sin(y)\\]for all $x,y\\in [0,\\pi]$.\nConsider the function $f(x,y)=\\sin(x)\\cos(y)+\\sin(y)\\cos(x)$. \nWe can compute $f_x(x,y)=\\cos(x)\\cos(y)-\\sin(x)\\sin(y)$ and $f_y(x,y)=\\cos(x)\\sin(y)+\\sin(x)\\cos(y)$.\nWe know that $f_x(x,y)\\leq0$ for all $x,y\\in [0,\\pi]$, so $f(x,y)$ is nonincreasing for $x$.\nFrom this, we know that $f(x,y)\\leq f(0,y)=\\sin(y)$ for all $x,y\\in [0,\\pi]$.\nThus, by the class of definition of $y^\\*$\n$$\\boxed{y\\in \\{y\\in [0,\\pi]: \\sin(y)\\leq \\sin(y)\\}}$$\n$=y\\in [0,\\pi].$\nThis implies that $\\sin(.5)=1/2$ is a perfect square root of a distance, \nand  beautiful.",
