    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We can draw the graphs.  Here is a sketch of a typical case from the yellow area:\n\nIt seems unlikely that one of the legs (of the red triangles) is noticeably above 90 degrees: $y$ in the $x+y\\leq\\sin(x)+\\sin(y)$ region is not large.\n\nThe question is, why is it so definitely?  One obvious possibility is:\n\\[\\sin(x+y)\\leq \\sin(x)+\\sin(y)\\]\n\\noindent\n\\noindent\n\\[\\ell \\cos(x-y) \\geq 0. \\notag\\]\n\\[\\ell \\cos(x-y) \\geq 0,\\, 0\\le x-y \\le \\pi. \\]\n\\[\\ell \\cos(x-y) = 0:\\quad ((\\tan x-\\tan y)^{2}-1)\\cos(x-y) = 0 \\notag. \\]\n\\[\\tan x - \\tan y = \\pm \\frac{1}{\\cos(x-y)}. \\]\n\\[\\tan x - \\tan y = -\\frac{1}{\\cos(x-y)} \\iff  \\tan x \\cos(x-y) - \\tan y \\sin(x-y) = -\\sin(x-y). \\]",
