    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The range of the sine function is $[-1,1]$.\nFor the inequality to hold for every $x$ between $0$ and $\\pi$, inclusive, it must hold for every $x$ in the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}]$.\nIn this case, the left side of the inequality is concave up and the right side is concave down.\nTheir normals intersect at the point $(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)$.\nSo, the function $\\sin(x)+\\sin(y)$ must lie below the normal to the function $\\sin(x+y)$ from above.\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent line at $(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)$.\nThe slope of the tangent line is $\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2})=-1$.\nSo, the slope of the normal is the negative reciprocal, which is $1$.\nTherefore, the function $\\sin(x+y)$ must lie below the line with slope $1$.\nThis occurs when $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq x+y\\leq \\pi$.\nThus, the values of $y$ that satisfy the inequality are $\\boxed{y\\in [0,0]}$.\nThe answer is: [0,0]",
